;; samizdat - a self-hosting agentic harness
;; SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later
;;
;; The branch phase machine, result transitions, and winner rubric as data
;; (drg-4026 #3/#30/#34 — audit findings 3, 30, 34). Loaded by
;; src/samizdat/agent/phases.clj, the wordlists precedent: state.clj sits
;; below gates.clj in the require graph, so state-level workflow data gets
;; its own loader rather than reading gates' accessor. Structure compiles
;; once at load where forms are involved (the rubric); tables are read at
;; fire time. Every section's WHY lives here beside the data.

{:initial-phase :explore

 ;; #34: the explore/build machine. A phase with a :cap-key is capped: past
 ;; the gates.edn threshold it names, the branch advances to :next and the
 ;; valve tells the model why (prompts/explore-cap.md). The cap VALUE stays a
 ;; gates.edn scalar the loop passes in — this table names which phase is
 ;; capped and what comes next, not how long it lasts.
 ;;
 ;; :withholds is the per-phase tool policy base/phase-refusal consults.
 ;; Empty: the proof harness's withheld tools left with its engines, and the
 ;; coding loop has no PHASE-locked tools. The refusals table below is the
 ;; one that is populated, because the rule that was missing turned on the
 ;; branch rather than on its phase. Both paths carry :policy-refusal? true
 ;; so a cull record can tell a declined call from a malformed fence.
 :phases {:explore {:cap-key :explore-cap :next :build :withholds #{}}
          :build {:withholds #{}}}

 ;; CONDITIONAL withholds: a refusal that depends on the BRANCH rather than
 ;; on its phase. :withholds above can only ask "which tools does this phase
 ;; forbid"; this table can ask anything about the branch, because a :when
 ;; form sees `branch` and `tool-name` as locals. Ordered, first match wins,
 ;; compiled once at load (a broken form fails there, not mid-run) while
 ;; everything the form reads is read at fire time.
 ;;
 ;; WHY THE FIRST ENTRY EXISTS. RFC-008 recorded that the task board was
 ;; "encouraged, not enforced": the per-turn context block says "No task
 ;; claimed" and the system prompt says work starts with a task, but nothing
 ;; refused a tool call from a branch holding none. Encouragement is not a
 ;; property — a model under pressure skips it exactly when the board would
 ;; have mattered most, and the board's whole purpose is arbitrating between
 ;; branches that are about to collide.
 ;;
 ;; WHICH TOOLS, and the three things this set is deliberately NOT.
 ;;
 ;; What the board arbitrates is who is CHANGING WHAT. On a team workflow the
 ;; competing implementors are branches of one run sharing one working tree,
 ;; and two of them editing the same file is the collision the board exists to
 ;; prevent. So the rule gates the two tools that mutate that tree, and
 ;; nothing else.
 ;;
 ;; NOT the investigating tools. `read_file`, `grep`, `lsp`, `shell` and
 ;; `eval` are how a branch finds out what needs doing — and a branch has to
 ;; be able to do that BEFORE it can know what to claim. A refusal on
 ;; `read_file` is a deadlock wearing a policy's clothes. `task` itself is
 ;; never withheld, for the same reason.
 ;;
 ;; NOT `done` or `give_up`. Refusing to let a branch FINISH because it never
 ;; claimed a task discards completed work to enforce bookkeeping, which is
 ;; the worst available trade: the work exists, the answer is in hand, and the
 ;; harness would be throwing it away over a missing row. Ending a run is also
 ;; where a bad refusal is least recoverable — there is no next turn to fix it
 ;; in. Every scripted end-to-end run drives `thesis` then `done`, and when
 ;; `done` was briefly in this set all of them failed; that was the mechanism
 ;; reporting a real design error, not a test fixture problem.
 ;;
 ;; NOT `eval`, specifically, on top of the above: the mutation protocol is
 ;; built on in-process eval (RFC-002), so withholding it would make the
 ;; harness's ability to modify itself depend on the task board.
 ;;
 ;; TO TURN IT OFF, set :tools to #{}. It is data, and a project that does not
 ;; want a board should not have to carry one.
 :refusals
 [{:rule :work-needs-a-task
   :when (nil? (:task branch))
   :tools #{"write_file" "edit_file"}
   :message-file "task-required"}

  ;; The storm guard (karamazov-ekk): withhold the identical repeat and the
  ;; A-B-A-B extension. Detection is samizdat.agent.storm (pure); thresholds
  ;; and tool classification are gates.edn (:storm-* thresholds, :storm-exempt
  ;; / :storm-mutating vocabularies); this table is only the DECISION that a
  ;; detected storm is withheld — remove a rule and the guard is advisory-off
  ;; for that shape, no rebuild. Ordered after the task rule so a task-less
  ;; repeated write is refused for the sharper reason. :tools :all because
  ;; exemption is the policy's business (the :storm-exempt vocabulary), not a
  ;; second list here to drift.
  {:rule :storm
   :when (samizdat.agent.storm/repeat-blocked?
          ctx (samizdat.agent.gates/storm-policy))
   :tools :all
   :message-file "storm"}

  {:rule :storm-oscillation
   :when (samizdat.agent.storm/oscillation-blocked?
          ctx (samizdat.agent.gates/storm-policy))
   :tools :all
   :message-file "storm-oscillation"}]

 ;; #3: tool-result signals → branch-state effects. The claim-first state
 ;; machine as a declarative table instead of cond-> clauses in the
 ;; executor. A key is a get-in path into the turn envelope
 ;; {:result <tool return> :artifact <produced artifact>}; when it holds a
 ;; truthy value, each named effect applies. Effect names dispatch in
 ;; loop.clj to state fns — a table cannot mutate the branch. The one
 ;; transition declared here is the green ship-verify: `done` reports
 ;; :verified-green? from its ship-verify rung, and green work is the green
 ;; point the safe-state rung rewinds to (it also ends a reframe — the
 ;; withheld approach could not have produced it, vf-9wx).
 ;;
 ;; A value can be matched, not just tested for truth: an entry whose value is
 ;; a MAP dispatches on what the path holds, `{:confirmed [...]}`. That is
 ;; what an artifact trigger needs — :claim-status is truthy for :confirmed,
 ;; :empirical and :sketch alike, so a truthy test would fire the confirmed
 ;; effects on an unverified plan. A status is a vocabulary, not a flag.
 ;;
 ;; The two entries ask DIFFERENT questions, which is why neither subsumes the
 ;; other. :mark-green keys on the verify signal, because the green point the
 ;; safe-state rung rewinds to is a fact about the WORKING TREE — the suite was
 ;; observed passing — and not about any claim. :clear-reframe keys on a
 ;; confirmed artifact, because that is what clear-reframe has always meant in
 ;; its own words: the branch banked something the withheld approach could not
 ;; have produced. Keying it on the artifact rather than on the verify signal
 ;; makes it general — ANY tool that confirms a claim ends a reframe, not only
 ;; a green ship-verify, which is the behaviour the docstring already
 ;; described and the table did not deliver.
 :transitions {[:result :verified-green?] [:mark-green]
               [:artifact :claim-status]  {:confirmed [:clear-reframe]}}

 ;; #30: the winner rubric — the ported UCLA FirstProof selector. EDN forms
 ;; compiled at load by state.clj into the ranking tuple, best-first
 ;; component order; the forms see `branch` as a local and the fns resolve
 ;; in state.clj. Components, most important first:
 ;; non-relaxation > slow-tier-seen > engine-diversity > confirmed-count >
 ;; id (a stable arbitrary tie-break so the ranking never depends on vector
 ;; order). A branch that proved the asked claim beats one that proved a
 ;; weakening; a cross-checked confirmation beats a one-shot; independent
 ;; engines compose (one Prolog + one Z3 beats two Z3s).
 :finished-key
 [(if (:relaxation? (:last-audit branch)) 0 1)
  (if (contains? (:tiers-seen branch) :slow) 1 0)
  (count (distinct (keep :kind (confirmed-artifacts branch))))
  (count (confirmed-artifacts branch))
  (:id branch)]}
